<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmed Sékou Touré</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sékou Touré</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmed Sékou Touré was a Guinean political leader and the first president of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. In 1958 he led Guinea to reject the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a> in a referendum, making it the first French colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a> to choose immediate independence. His stand is a notable episode in the decline of French colonial governance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmed-sekou-toure.jpg" alt="Ahmed Sékou Touré" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Knudsen. White House Photographs. John F. Kennedy Presidential Library and Museum, Boston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmed_Seko%C3%BA_in_1962_at_White_House_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
